gates near D+4.5", "Ramaswamy's roughly 4-to-1 cash advantage plus Trump-Vance surrogate blitz could tighten the race", "Democratic-tilting midterm environment offsets much of Ohio's R+5 lean in an open seat", "Markets price Acton near 70 percent while most raters still call it a Tossup"], "rationale": "Nonpartisan and academic pollsters uniformly show Acton ahead by four to nine points and prediction markets price her near 70 percent, but Ramaswamy's dominant fundraising and the Trump-Vance campaign stops in the closing weeks give him room to close; Ohio's Republican-leaning electorate keeps the expected margin modest.", "watch": "Whether Ramaswamy's advertising blitz and GOP surrogate visits visibly narrow Acton's polling lead over the final two weeks.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Mike DeWine (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "An open-seat race to succeed the term-limited Mike DeWine pits Democrat Amy Acton against Republican Vivek Ramaswamy. Polling since mid-September consistently shows Acton ahead: high-quality, nonpartisan surveys give her leads of four to nine points, while Republican-sponsored polls show a roughly even contest. Aggregated averages across major trackers sit near Acton plus four and a half, and the most recent survey in early October still favored her.\n\nRamaswamy holds a commanding money advantage, with about $30.7 million on hand to Acton's $8.1 million, though the gap has been closing, and Trump and Vance have blitzed Ohio in the final stretch. Most rating outlets call the race a Tossup, while prediction markets put Acton's chances near 70 percent. Ohio's Republican-leaning electorate and Ramaswamy's resources keep this genuinely competitive, but the polling picture and a favorable midterm environment give Acton a modest edge.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.545, "margin": 0.49}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.5929, "margin": 1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.5919, "margin": 1.0}, {"date"